Subject: An overflow of the stack in compiled code builds its backtrace where it is thrown

What this does
  - `qjs_aot_throw_stack_overflow` throws the RangeError with its backtrace built at the throw.

Why we need it
  An error the engine throws from a bytecode function gets its backtrace later, when the exception is
  caught and the unwinder of that function runs (`qjs_aot_unwind`). For a compiled function with no
  frame (a frameless body), the activation records that stand for it and its callers are popped as the
  exception returns through them, so by then the trace held only the frame that caught it: a deep
  recursion that overflowed printed `at <eval>` where the interpreter prints the recursing function ten
  times. Built at the throw, the trace has the whole chain of records, and the first line is the call
  that overflowed, the same as the interpreter's.

--- a/quickjs.c
+++ b/quickjs.c
@@ -64996,13 +64996,27 @@
 #endif
 }
 
+static JSValue JS_PRINTF_FORMAT_ATTR(2, 3)
+qjs_aot_throw_range_error_bt(JSContext *ctx, JS_PRINTF_FORMAT const char *fmt, ...)
+{
+    va_list ap;
+    JSValue ret;
+
+    va_start(ap, fmt);
+    ret = JS_ThrowError2(ctx, JS_RANGE_ERROR, true, fmt, ap);
+    va_end(ap);
+    return ret;
+}
+
 /* A native entry checks the C stack itself, so a deep recursion of compiled
    functions raises the interpreter's own RangeError instead of running into
    the guard page. The message and the constructor must match the
    interpreter's exactly: a test compares them. */
 QJS_AOT_STUB JSValue qjs_aot_throw_stack_overflow(JSContext *ctx)
 {
-    return JS_ThrowStackOverflow(ctx);
+    /* The backtrace is built here, not when the exception is caught: the frameless
+       activation records that stand for the calling functions are gone by then. */
+    return qjs_aot_throw_range_error_bt(ctx, "Maximum call stack size exceeded");
 }
 
